1
00:00:00,206 --> 00:00:08,400
So I've teamed up with Anthropic, who is sponsoring this video, and I am so excited about this because this is the video that I wish I had just a couple of years ago.

2
00:00:08,526 --> 00:00:13,764
And that's because back then I kept hearing about AI agents, agentic AI, subagents.

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:17,520
And I remember thinking to myself, what in the world am I supposed to build with any of this?

4
00:00:17,838 --> 00:00:22,852
Well, if you fast forward to today, I built over 150 AI agents that completely run my personal life.

5
00:00:23,022 --> 00:00:28,560
And in this video, I want to walk you through building your very first one, which is a to-do list agent.

6
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:32,640
Now, this one's one of my favorites because it absolutely changed the way that I work with AI.

7
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:40,480
And by the end of this video, you're going to see that not only is your agent working on your computer, but it's also working on your phone, even if your computer is turned off.

8
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:45,680
But before we build anything, let's go ahead and get started with what an AI agent actually is.

9
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:49,040
Now, most people use Claude just by chatting with it, and I think that's good.

10
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,800
For example, let's say that you're going to ask Claude to help you plan a birthday party.

11
00:00:52,910 --> 00:01:01,828
Well, it's going to give you a great list of ideas, and then you're going to take those ideas, and then you're going to book a place for your birthday, you're going to order your birthday cake, and then you're going to send out invites.

12
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,360
Claude was the helper here, but you still did all the work.

13
00:01:05,582 --> 00:01:07,076
An AI agent is different.

14
00:01:07,374 --> 00:01:12,324
With an agent, you're going to give Claude a goal instead of a question, and then it's going to figure out the steps.

15
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:19,440
In fact, it's going to use the right tools to do the work, it's going to check on its own work, and then it's going to keep going until the job is actually done.

16
00:01:19,630 --> 00:01:22,080
You might also have heard the term agentic AI.

17
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:28,240
Well, agentic AI is just a bigger umbrella for any AI that takes action and uses the tools for you.

18
00:01:28,494 --> 00:01:31,428
But a term that you may not hear all the time is subagents.

19
00:01:31,534 --> 00:01:33,200
And subagents are just helpers.

20
00:01:33,422 --> 00:01:37,280
And you can think of it like this: let's say that the main agent has a task to complete.

21
00:01:37,390 --> 00:01:42,500
Well, it can break that task into smaller pieces, then hand them out to as many subagents as it needs.

22
00:01:42,702 --> 00:01:48,960
And that means that each subagent is going to go off and do its own piece of its work, and then it's going to report back to the main agent.

23
00:01:49,102 --> 00:01:52,160
And then that main agent is going to put it all together at the end.

24
00:01:52,366 --> 00:01:57,520
Okay, with that said, and those definitions out of the way, let's go ahead and get started and set up your very first agent.

25
00:01:57,678 --> 00:02:00,640
Now I'm in the Claude desktop app right now, as you can see.

26
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:05,360
And up here at the top, you see that there's a switch that I can change it from chat to code.

27
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:07,920
And when I switch it, you see that this is clawed code.

28
00:02:08,142 --> 00:02:10,880
You also can see that there's no terminal and there's no coding.

29
00:02:11,086 --> 00:02:14,560
And we are going to build this whole agent system right inside of the Claude app.

30
00:02:14,734 --> 00:02:18,080
Now, quick note on that clawed code does require a paid plan.

31
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:18,960
So keep that in mind.

32
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:26,880
Now to start here, right underneath the prompt box, I'm going to select local, which means that I'm going to be running this on my local computer just for now.

33
00:02:27,086 --> 00:02:28,400
And then I need to pick a folder.

34
00:02:28,526 --> 00:02:33,200
I've already made an empty folder, as you can see right here, which is called to do agent.

35
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:37,120
And for the brain or the model, I'm going to keep it on Opus 5.5.

36
00:02:37,326 --> 00:02:45,360
And that's because Anthropic says that it performs at the same level as Fable 5.1 on most of its work and it's using less of our weekly or monthly limit.

37
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,828
Now the last setting here is mode, and we are going to use auto mode to build this out.

38
00:02:49,998 --> 00:02:52,740
But before we build anything out, we're also going to use plan.

39
00:02:53,326 --> 00:02:55,040
So we'll learn about that here just in a second.

40
00:02:55,214 --> 00:03:02,640
But when we select auto, Claude is going to automatically approve the things that it knows is safe, and then it's going to block anything that it deems risky.

41
00:03:02,830 --> 00:03:12,320
So what that means is that it's not going to ask for permission every five seconds, but it also has really strong safety guardrails in place so we can feel confident as it builds this out for us.

42
00:03:12,558 --> 00:03:20,160
Now there is one thing you should do first if this is the first time that you've ever made an AI agent, and that is creating a Claude.md file.

43
00:03:20,334 --> 00:03:23,680
If you've never used Claude before, it doesn't know anything about you yet.

44
00:03:23,886 --> 00:03:26,720
It doesn't know what you like, it doesn't know how you want things done.

45
00:03:26,926 --> 00:03:30,240
And because of that, the very first thing you're going to want to do is tell it what you like.

46
00:03:30,574 --> 00:03:33,072
Matter of fact, I told it that I like everything to look premium.

47
00:03:33,166 --> 00:03:34,912
I told it that I like shorter messages.

48
00:03:35,086 --> 00:03:38,112
I told it to never send anything without asking me first.

49
00:03:38,350 --> 00:03:39,632
These are things that it should know.

50
00:03:39,854 --> 00:03:48,740
And if you take a look right here, Claude wrote all of that down in a file called Claude.md, which means that every time you come back to this project, Claude is going to read that file first.

51
00:03:48,942 --> 00:03:52,352
So you never have to repeat yourself and it already understands your preferences.

52
00:03:52,622 --> 00:03:59,952
And the cool thing that I like about the Claude.md file is that anytime that you're correcting Claude is going to remember that and put it inside of that file.

53
00:04:00,142 --> 00:04:05,472
And that is why when we start building out this agent, you're gonna notice that it already understands the way that I like things done.

54
00:04:05,632 --> 00:04:11,396
But just know that the more that you use Claude, the more it's gonna understand you and understand your preferences.

55
00:04:11,662 --> 00:04:14,992
So to get things going here, we are gonna switch over to plan mode.

56
00:04:15,072 --> 00:04:19,232
And in plan mode, Claude can't touch anything until you approve the plan.

57
00:04:19,392 --> 00:04:23,952
What plan mode is going to do is it's going to think through the prompt thoroughly that you gave it.

58
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:27,204
It's gonna do that first, and then it's gonna ask you questions.

59
00:04:27,502 --> 00:04:32,112
And then it's gonna show you exactly what's going to build before it actually builds anything out.

60
00:04:32,206 --> 00:04:38,752
And to make this significantly faster, what we're gonna do is we're gonna click on this little record button right here and we are just going to talk.

61
00:04:38,992 --> 00:04:42,032
Researchers found that talking is about three times faster than typing.

62
00:04:42,112 --> 00:04:43,312
And I do have a rule.

63
00:04:43,406 --> 00:04:50,832
And that rule is called the 30-second rule, which means that you have to talk to Claude for at least 30 seconds before you ask it to do anything.

64
00:04:51,054 --> 00:04:55,392
And that's because talking will give it way more detail than you ever could if you were just typing.

65
00:04:55,598 --> 00:05:01,792
So since we're building this to-do list agent, what I did was I just brain dumped everything I could think of into Claude.

66
00:05:01,952 --> 00:05:04,512
And that was things like what I think the agent should do.

67
00:05:04,782 --> 00:05:07,460
I told it that I wanted to have three piles for our tasks.

68
00:05:07,790 --> 00:05:09,188
I wanted to use subagents.

69
00:05:09,422 --> 00:05:12,992
And then I want nothing to go out without it asking me first.

70
00:05:13,262 --> 00:05:17,712
And I also told it that I want a dashboard that looks like it cost $10,000 to make.

71
00:05:17,902 --> 00:05:21,152
And I like that as a little tip because it looks a little bit more premium when we do that.

72
00:05:21,358 --> 00:05:26,672
And because we're in plan mode, Claude is going to come back with a few questions so it fully understands what I'm asking for.

73
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:31,712
And it asks me things like, Do I want a dashboard or when should it start my to-do list?

74
00:05:31,822 --> 00:05:34,912
And I just told it that I want a button that says get it done.

75
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:37,712
That means when I put in my tasks, I can click get it done.

76
00:05:37,934 --> 00:05:41,072
And when it's done with the questions, it is going to start writing the plan.

77
00:05:41,326 --> 00:05:45,312
And once done with the plan, if you take a look right here, it did address everything that I've asked for.

78
00:05:45,518 --> 00:05:49,632
And it's explaining exactly how it's going to build this out and test its own work.

79
00:05:49,774 --> 00:05:56,752
Now, if I like the plan, I can click accept and switch back to auto, which means it's going to switch back to auto mode and then I can watch it build out.

80
00:05:56,912 --> 00:06:02,192
Now this part does take a few minutes to build out, but when it's done, you can see that the dashboard is on the right hand side.

81
00:06:02,350 --> 00:06:07,552
And when I first saw it, it did have everything that I asked for, but it doesn't look premium to me.

82
00:06:07,854 --> 00:06:08,752
So I told Claude that.

83
00:06:08,912 --> 00:06:15,872
I told Claude that I like big elements, I like bright colors, and I wanted to think about it differently because it really isn't hitting the mark.

84
00:06:16,046 --> 00:06:18,192
And the second time around, this is what came back.

85
00:06:18,446 --> 00:06:22,752
First of all, Claude is calling my agent Day Book, and I do like the way that this looks.

86
00:06:22,990 --> 00:06:25,632
And if you take a look at the top here, you will see a pretty cool feature.

87
00:06:25,902 --> 00:06:29,552
And that feature is going to show us how much time it saved us for the day.

88
00:06:29,632 --> 00:06:31,952
It's also going to show us the tasks that I finished for the week.

89
00:06:32,174 --> 00:06:38,112
And it's also going to give me my delegation score, which is how much of the list my agent was able to complete for me.

90
00:06:38,414 --> 00:06:44,192
And at the bottom here, it's even going to suggest different tasks that I can add based off of the tasks that I already gave it.

91
00:06:44,366 --> 00:06:45,872
But right now the dashboard is empty.

92
00:06:45,952 --> 00:06:48,228
So let's go ahead and give it our actual to-do list.

93
00:06:48,432 --> 00:06:53,072
Before we do that, really quick, if this has been helpful so far, please like the video, click subscribe.

94
00:06:53,326 --> 00:06:56,352
It really helps out the channel and it's something that I really appreciate.

95
00:06:56,512 --> 00:06:58,432
But let's get back to the agent.

96
00:06:58,512 --> 00:07:00,832
Okay, now I have a lot of things that need to get done here.

97
00:07:00,992 --> 00:07:02,912
So I'm just going to start typing them in.

98
00:07:03,118 --> 00:07:07,652
And I want to type in things like I need a haircut for me and my son, so go ahead and schedule that for me.

99
00:07:07,918 --> 00:07:10,832
I need new clothes for the fall because I hate to shop.

100
00:07:11,182 --> 00:07:12,464
And then Christmas is coming up.

101
00:07:12,544 --> 00:07:16,196
So I want you to reach out to my family and figure out what they want for Christmas.

102
00:07:16,462 --> 00:07:19,504
And of course, there's five or six others that I put in here as well.

103
00:07:19,726 --> 00:07:24,064
But as you can see, as I start to add them, they're already sorting them into piles.

104
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:28,704
But when you're ready and you're done typing in your to-do list, there's a button that says get it done.

105
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:31,904
When you click that, it's going to start working on those tasks for you.

106
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:40,944
Now what's happening here is that the main agent is going to hand each task to different subagents, and they are all gonna work at the same time to make sure that things get done.

107
00:07:41,104 --> 00:07:46,304
And at the very end, there is an agent that is going to check the work before it marks everything complete.

108
00:07:46,542 --> 00:07:50,980
Now, just a heads up, running a bunch of subagents at the same time uses more of your limit.

109
00:07:51,214 --> 00:07:54,784
So if you're just starting out here, maybe just try a few tasks at first.

110
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:59,904
The one thing I think that's really cool here is what we've done is we've created something called an artifact.

111
00:08:00,078 --> 00:08:09,184
An artifact is basically a page or a little app that Claude builds for you, and then it saves it in your Claude account, meaning that it's sitting in Claude's cloud.

112
00:08:09,390 --> 00:08:11,844
And that means that we can access it from any device.

113
00:08:12,174 --> 00:08:21,984
So what I'm going to do here so I'm going to pick up my phone and I'm going to go to the Claude app and I'm going to click on menu and then tap on artifacts, and you are going to see Daybook.

114
00:08:22,158 --> 00:08:27,024
And that means that I have the same access to this tool that's on my computer, but now on my phone.

115
00:08:27,278 --> 00:08:31,024
And it doesn't matter if your computer is on or not, because this is living in the cloud.

116
00:08:31,214 --> 00:08:33,956
And not only that, you can add more tasks from your phone.

117
00:08:34,144 --> 00:08:38,784
It acts just like it was on your computer because it's the same file, it's just in the cloud.

118
00:08:39,022 --> 00:08:42,384
For example, I'm going to tell it that it wanted to build me a 2027 planner.

119
00:08:42,606 --> 00:08:46,340
And then when I click enter, you can see that it goes directly on the list to be worked.

120
00:08:46,574 --> 00:08:52,224
Now, as we go down the list here, you can see that there's one task that I really don't want the agent to finish without asking me first.

121
00:08:52,384 --> 00:08:53,892
And that is the Christmas task.

122
00:08:54,190 --> 00:08:56,464
And that is sitting in the together pile.

123
00:08:56,654 --> 00:08:59,844
Now I already have my Gmail connector connected with Claude.

124
00:09:00,142 --> 00:09:04,612
So instead of sending emails automatically, what I had to do was create drafts.

125
00:09:04,878 --> 00:09:09,636
